WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.635
Serialization review should be repeatable.

2
00:00:02.715 --> 00:00:06.197
Code review should identify
ObjectInputStream, Serializable classes,

3
00:00:06.277 --> 00:00:09.086
readObject methods, deserialization
filters, JSON, XML,

4
00:00:09.166 --> 00:00:12.873
and YAML parser configuration, polymorphic
binding, custom deserializers,

5
00:00:12.953 --> 00:00:16.267
message DTOs, raw payload logging, and
direct entity serialization.

6
00:00:16.347 --> 00:00:19.493
Reviewers should ask what is being
deserialized, who supplied it,

7
00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:23.449
which classes or DTOs are allowed, what
limits apply, and what happens on failure.

8
00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:26.225
Tests should cover malformed payloads,
unknown fields,

9
00:00:26.305 --> 00:00:29.169
unsupported versions, oversized data,
deeply nested data,

10
00:00:29.249 --> 00:00:32.676
rejected classes, unauthorized messages,
sensitive field exclusion,

11
00:00:32.756 --> 00:00:33.992
and safe error responses.

12
00:00:34.072 --> 00:00:37.610
Fuzzing can be useful for parsers, binary
formats, custom deserializers,

13
00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.207
and file or message boundaries.

14
00:00:39.287 --> 00:00:41.478
These tests are not only about parser
crashes.

15
00:00:41.558 --> 00:00:45.658
They verify that the application rejects
unexpected state before it becomes business

16
00:00:45.738 --> 00:00:46.244
behavior.

17
00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:49.245
CI/CD should run tests, dependency checks,
secret scanning,

18
00:00:49.325 --> 00:00:50.785
static analysis where useful,

19
00:00:50.865 --> 00:00:54.067
and review dependency changes that affect
serializers or parsers.

20
00:00:54.147 --> 00:00:57.405
A new library version can change parser
defaults, subtype handling,

21
00:00:57.485 --> 00:00:59.844
XML behavior, YAML construction, or DTO
mapping.

22
00:00:59.924 --> 00:01:03.968
Dependency review matters because
serialization behavior often lives in framework

23
00:01:04.048 --> 00:01:06.632
integration code rather than obvious
business logic.

24
00:01:06.712 --> 00:01:09.521
Incident response should include affected
parser review,

25
00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:13.027
classpath dependency review, secret
rotation when needed, log review,

26
00:01:13.107 --> 00:01:17.320
message replay review, cache and
dead-letter inspection, and remediation validation.

27
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.770
Serialization paths should have owners and
documented expectations.

28
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.165
During an incident, someone needs to know
which inputs were accepted,

29
00:01:24.245 --> 00:01:26.997
which fields were logged, which messages
can be replayed,

30
00:01:27.077 --> 00:01:29.380
and which downstream actions may have
occurred.
